      "rule": "R7",
      "severity": "must",
      "text": "The aggregate-query check accepts `SELECT 1 FROM t HAVING count(*) > 0` and `SELECT 1 FROM t ORDER BY count(*)`, although SQLite rejects both. Fix: classify queries from `GROUP BY` and result-column aggregates, enforce SQLite's clause-specific errors, and add regression cases."
    },
    {
      "id": "20261021T030629Z-4a951d58#2",
      "line": 67,
      "path": "packages/belay-sqlite/Belay/Sqlite/Resolution/QueryAggregates.lean",
      "rule": "R7",
      "severity": "must",
      "text": "The grouping-term check accepts a scalar output containing an aggregate, so `SELECT count(*) + 1 FROM t GROUP BY 1` succeeds instead of reporting SQLite's aggregate-in-GROUP-BY error. Fix: reject referenced result expressions that contain aggregates and add a regression case."
    },
    {
      "id": "20261021T030629Z-4a951d58#3",
      "line": 34,
      "path": "packages/belay-sqlite/Belay/Sqlite/Resolution/SyntaxEquality.lean",
      "rule": "R5",
      "severity": "must",
      "text": "The catch-all in `Syntax.Expr.same` silently treats any new expression constructor as unequal instead of requiring its implementation. Fix: enumerate the expression constructors explicitly."
    },
    {
      "id": "20261021T030629Z-4a951d58#4",
      "line": 58,
      "path": "packages/belay-sqlite/Belay/Sqlite/Resolution/QueryAggregates.lean",
      "rule": "R5",
      "severity": "must",
      "text": "The catch-all in `resolveAggregateCall` hides new expression constructors from exhaustiveness checking. Fix: enumerate the remaining expression constructors explicitly."
    },
